<article class="env-proposition" id="04PL">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04PL">Proposition <span data-tag="04PL">9.6.8.17</span> <span class="named">(Lifting Factorization Systems)</span>.</a> Let $U: \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a cartesian fibration of $\infty $-categories and let $(S_{L}, S_{R} )$ be a factorization system on $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Let $\widetilde{S}_{L}$ denote the collection of all morphisms $\widetilde{f}$ in $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ satisfying $U( \widetilde{f} ) \in S_{L}$, and let $\widetilde{S}_{R}$ be the collection of all $U$-cartesian morphisms $\widetilde{g}$ in $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ satisfying $U( \widetilde{g}) \in S_{R}$. Then the pair $( \widetilde{S}_{L}, \widetilde{S}_{R} )$ is a factorization system on $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Since the collections $S_{L}$ and $S_{R}$ are closed under isomorphism, the collections $\widetilde{S}_{L}$ and $\widetilde{S}_{R}$ have the same property. It follows from Corollary <a data-tag="06WL" href="/tag/06WL">9.6.6.23</a> that $\widetilde{S}_{L}$ is left orthogonal to $\widetilde{S}_{R}$. We will complete the proof by showing that every morphism $\widetilde{h}: \widetilde{X} \rightarrow \widetilde{Z}$ in $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ factors as a composition $\widetilde{g} \circ \widetilde{f}$, where $\widetilde{f}$ belongs to $\widetilde{S}_{L}$ and $\widetilde{g}$ belongs to $\widetilde{S}_{R}$. Set $h = U(\widetilde{h} )$. Since$(S_{L}, S_{R} )$ is a factorization system, we can choose a $2$-simplex $\sigma :$ 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ &amp;  Y \ar [dr]^{g} &amp;  \\ X \ar [ur]^{f} \ar [rr]^{h} &amp;  &amp;  Z } \]
</div>
<p>
       of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, where $f$ belongs to $S_{L}$ and $g$ belongs to $S_{R}$. Our assumption that $U$ is a cartesian fibration guarantees that we can lift $g$ to a $U$-cocartesian morphism $\widetilde{g}: \widetilde{Y} \rightarrow \widetilde{Z}$ in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$. Since $\widetilde{g}$ is $U$-cocartesian, we can lift $\sigma $ to a $2$-simplex $\widetilde{\sigma }:$ 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ &amp;  \widetilde{Y} \ar [dr]^{\widetilde{g}} &amp;  \\ \widetilde{X} \ar [ur]^{\widetilde{f}} \ar [rr]^{\widetilde{h}} &amp;  &amp;  \widetilde{Z} } \]
</div>
<p>
       in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$. By construction, we have $\widetilde{f} \in \widetilde{S}_{L}$ and $\widetilde{g} \in \widetilde{S}_{R}$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></p></p></article>
